WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.505 --> 00:00:08.329
Hello, welcome back to Arya Nagarjuna,

00:00:08.329 --> 00:00:09.844
class number 15.

00:00:09.844 --> 00:00:14.844
We were reviewing these chapters
that we were going through

00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:18.721
because we were trying to
get a picture of the flow

00:00:18.721 --> 00:00:22.919
of all the chapters, which
we didn't get when we were

00:00:22.919 --> 00:00:25.307
focusing on individual hard ideas.

00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:28.439
And so we were talking about...

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:33.189
We're talking just now
about somebody complaining

00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:37.717
to Arya Nagarjuna and saying,

00:00:37.717 --> 00:00:41.444
"Well, if these things don't
exist from their own side

00:00:41.444 --> 00:00:44.111
"then why didn't Buddha say that?

00:00:45.174 --> 00:00:47.992
"And why did he say things
did exist from their own side

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:49.297
for a long time?"

00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:53.073
And then Arya Nagarjuna has
pointed out that they...

00:00:53.073 --> 00:00:55.201
And as our audience said here...

00:00:55.201 --> 00:00:56.302
Who was that?

00:00:56.302 --> 00:00:58.066
It depended on the students.

00:00:58.066 --> 00:01:01.730
It's just up to the what students he had.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.505
It's very interesting that Choney Lama

00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:07.684
before he moves on to the next topic,

00:01:07.684 --> 00:01:11.795
he goes to something that
is a big favorite of ours,

00:01:11.795 --> 00:01:14.295
which is... Tim, the next picture!

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.378
"The Diamond Cutter Sutra."

00:01:20.378 --> 00:01:22.802
And I love...this is
very famous, this is the

00:01:22.802 --> 00:01:24.840
"The Diamond Cutter Sutra"

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.943
in Chinese, the oldest
dated book in the world

00:01:27.943 --> 00:01:32.246
with a date inside of it,
which I think is 880AD.

00:01:32.246 --> 00:01:37.103
And why we brought it up
here is that Choney Lama,

00:01:37.103 --> 00:01:40.571
who wrote the great commentary
to "Diamond Cutter Sutra,"

00:01:40.571 --> 00:01:43.571
which you should buy online as an e-book

00:01:45.677 --> 00:01:49.927
from Diamond Cutter Press,
because it just came out.

00:01:52.758 --> 00:01:57.074
It's on his mind. It's on
Choney Lama's mind. And he says,

00:01:57.074 --> 00:02:00.556
"By the way, while you're on the subject

00:02:00.556 --> 00:02:05.196
"of complaining about what
Buddha said or didn't say,

00:02:05.196 --> 00:02:08.823
"do you remember in the
Diamond Cutter Sutra,

00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:11.323
he says Buddhas don't teach."

00:02:12.668 --> 00:02:15.923
Yes, he said, "Don't complain to me,

00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:18.127
"Buddha said Buddhas don't teach.

00:02:18.127 --> 00:02:20.138
"Don't complain about the content,

00:02:20.138 --> 00:02:21.588
"complain about the fact he said

00:02:21.588 --> 00:02:23.598
I shouldn't be teaching at all."

00:02:23.598 --> 00:02:28.217
And again, of course, that
means that as we know,

00:02:28.217 --> 00:02:33.217
as we've talked about many
times, when you read this sutra

00:02:33.449 --> 00:02:37.750
or any other sutra, actually
what's happening is your

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.213
mind is projecting the
words down on a blank page

00:02:41.213 --> 00:02:43.550
and in a way you can say you're the author

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.594
of "The Diamond Cutter Sutra."

00:02:45.594 --> 00:02:49.291
Does that mean you understand
it? Not necessarily.

00:02:49.291 --> 00:02:54.257
Well, how can I be writing a
book that I don't understand?

00:02:54.257 --> 00:02:59.257
Karma expands 100% every day.
You figure out the numbers.

00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:02.531
What's interest rate? It's
like a million or something,

00:03:02.531 --> 00:03:03.697
I don't know.

00:03:03.697 --> 00:03:07.114
So, there is a famous statement

00:03:07.949 --> 00:03:10.102
in "The Diamond Cutter Sutra",

00:03:10.102 --> 00:03:13.071
that when the Buddha
teaches they don't teach.

00:03:13.071 --> 00:03:17.231
And what that means is
when the Buddha does teach,

00:03:17.231 --> 00:03:20.128
that's coming from you.

00:03:20.128 --> 00:03:22.326
All right, we said there
were five big topics

00:03:22.326 --> 00:03:24.175
in the 'me' and 'mine' stuff.

00:03:24.175 --> 00:03:26.191
Here's the third one.

00:03:26.191 --> 00:03:29.556
DE KHO NA NYID LA BKRI BA'I RIM PA,

00:03:29.556 --> 00:03:32.970
which means they get
into a small discussion

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.653
of what's the best way for us

00:03:34.653 --> 00:03:37.192
to teach other people these ideas.

00:03:37.192 --> 00:03:40.828
And that got into some different ideas

00:03:40.828 --> 00:03:44.439
about the meaning of the word 'pure'.

00:03:44.439 --> 00:03:46.512
BKRI here B-K-R-I,

00:03:46.512 --> 00:03:49.037
means when it comes time for us

00:03:49.037 --> 00:03:51.636
to teach other people emptiness

00:03:51.636 --> 00:03:54.058
then what are his suggestions?

00:03:54.058 --> 00:03:55.724
And he basically says the same thing.

00:03:55.724 --> 00:03:58.718
He says, "Teach them
according to their capacity

00:03:58.718 --> 00:04:00.941
and don't go over their capacity."

00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:05.130
Fourth step about examining me and mine

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.673
is that it's called

00:04:06.673 --> 00:04:08.884
DE KHO NA NYID KYI MTSAN NYID,

00:04:08.884 --> 00:04:11.551
which means what are the main...

00:04:13.095 --> 00:04:15.073
I always think of a nice car.

00:04:15.073 --> 00:04:19.201
Seiji's just bought a
Tesla which I'm looking at,

00:04:19.201 --> 00:04:21.568
because it's so pretty.

00:04:21.568 --> 00:04:24.600
I just don't know if I want to sit on

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.183
two tons of electromagnetic
cancer... Anyway.

00:04:32.627 --> 00:04:34.719
DE KHO NA NYID KYI MTSAN NYID,

00:04:34.719 --> 00:04:38.061
means when you're selling a Tesla you say,

00:04:38.061 --> 00:04:40.984
"Look it's got this beautiful front end,

00:04:40.984 --> 00:04:43.954
"and it's got this navigation system.

00:04:43.954 --> 00:04:46.731
"It's got special brakes, like
when you let off the brakes

00:04:46.731 --> 00:04:50.712
"they put on the brakes, and
stuff like when you go around

00:04:50.712 --> 00:04:52.395
"a curve and you let off the gas,

00:04:52.395 --> 00:04:55.145
"it puts on proportional braking.

00:04:57.304 --> 00:05:00.714
So you kind of only drive with one foot."

00:05:00.714 --> 00:05:04.691
So anyway, those are the
selling points of the car.

00:05:04.691 --> 00:05:06.654
Those are the cool things about the car.

00:05:06.654 --> 00:05:08.586
He says, "Hey you want
to know the cool things

00:05:08.586 --> 00:05:09.927
"about emptiness?

00:05:09.927 --> 00:05:12.330
Let me tell you the selling
points of emptiness."

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.640
And that's the fourth
main topic here called,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.291
DE KHO NA NYID KYI MTSAN NYID,

00:05:16.291 --> 00:05:20.117
which means, "I'll give you some
selling points for emptiness"

00:05:20.117 --> 00:05:24.000
and the verse is very important,
the verse is very famous.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.583
You got this verse?

00:05:26.422 --> 00:05:27.489
GZHAN LA...

00:05:27.489 --> 00:05:31.434
Yes, boy, there was a big
a mistake in the Tibetan.

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:36.300
Not the input operators, it was
a mistake of the carver. But

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.854
I'm going to just read this
to you because is very famous.

00:05:40.854 --> 00:05:41.687
,GZHAN LAS SHES MIN ZHI BA DANG,

00:05:41.687 --> 00:05:42.520
,SPROS PA RNAMS KYIS MA SPROS PA,

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.353
,RNAM RTOG MED DON THA DAD MIN,

00:05:43.353 --> 00:05:48.353
,DE NI DE NYID MTSAN NYID DO,,

00:05:48.895 --> 00:05:51.450
Last line says DE NI
DE NYID MTSAN NYID DO,,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.483
means, "Hey you want to
know the big selling points

00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:54.697
for emptiness?"

00:05:54.697 --> 00:05:56.204
Which here he calls,

00:05:56.204 --> 00:05:58.157
DE NYID, which is tatva,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:00.121
which means suchness.

00:06:00.121 --> 00:06:03.233
Let me give you the main
selling points for suchness.

00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:06.043
And then he says, it's an amazing list.

00:06:06.043 --> 00:06:10.800
GZHAN LAS SHES MIN means no one...

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.488
It's not something you can
learn from somebody else.

00:06:14.488 --> 00:06:16.685
It's not something you can
learn from somebody else.

00:06:16.685 --> 00:06:19.005
Then Geshe-la, why everyday of the year

00:06:19.005 --> 00:06:23.755
you're sitting here talking
about emptiness, making videos?

00:06:24.905 --> 00:06:28.645
It cannot be communicated
so directly that a person

00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:30.988
sees emptiness right
after you talk to them.

00:06:30.988 --> 00:06:33.082
It's not possible. It never happens.

00:06:33.082 --> 00:06:35.447
Well, it almost happens.

00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:39.620
But it never happens
that you can meet someone

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.395
who saw emptiness directly and say,

00:06:42.395 --> 00:06:45.201
"Look just give me five
minutes, let's sit down,

00:06:45.201 --> 00:06:48.334
you just tell me what
you saw and that's it."

00:06:48.334 --> 00:06:50.876
And he says, "No", GZHAN LAS SHES MIN

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:52.878
It's not something that you can learn

00:06:52.878 --> 00:06:55.638
from another person immediately.

00:06:55.638 --> 00:06:58.298
They need to teach you over, over, over

00:06:58.298 --> 00:07:00.725
and then you'll have the experience.

00:07:00.725 --> 00:07:01.607
ZHI BA...

00:07:01.607 --> 00:07:06.241
And I'm going to define this
different selling points.

00:07:06.241 --> 00:07:08.491
There are five of them:

00:07:09.421 --> 00:07:12.588
five Tesla's selling points.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.490
The first one is,

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:18.691
GZHAN GYIS BSTAN PA TZAM
LAS DNGOS SU SHES PA MIN GYI

00:07:18.691 --> 00:07:20.396
You cannot have the direct experience

00:07:20.396 --> 00:07:21.835
just by listening to someone

00:07:21.835 --> 00:07:24.903
describe it, even a direct experience.

00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:28.027
GNYIS SNANG LOG PA'I
TSUL GYIS MTHONG DGOS PA

00:07:28.027 --> 00:07:29.659
You must work on your habit

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:32.539
of misunderstanding where
things come from. That's all.

00:07:32.539 --> 00:07:35.249
You have to work on the
pen, work on the pen.

00:07:35.249 --> 00:07:38.310
Then it will be communicated to you.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:43.108
But no matter how much a
person would love to sit down

00:07:43.108 --> 00:07:46.247
and describe it to you, it's not possible.

00:07:46.247 --> 00:07:48.372
It just never happens.

00:07:48.372 --> 00:07:52.018
You don't have that many seeds.

00:07:52.018 --> 00:07:54.522
First selling point, which
is kind of a negative thing

00:07:54.522 --> 00:07:55.409
for me.

00:07:55.409 --> 00:07:57.365
You can't learn it just by listening

00:07:57.365 --> 00:07:58.509
to someone for 10 minutes.

00:07:58.509 --> 00:08:00.228
It's not going to happen.

00:08:00.228 --> 00:08:04.311
Second, then it says 'peace',
and then you're like,

00:08:06.988 --> 00:08:10.343
"Oh, if I study emptiness
I will enjoy my life.

00:08:10.343 --> 00:08:15.254
"I'll always sit around
on my couch and feel good

00:08:15.254 --> 00:08:17.171
even without Netflix,"

00:08:19.012 --> 00:08:20.531
which is almost inconceivable.
But 'the peace' here

00:08:20.531 --> 00:08:25.531
is not the peace that means to feel good.

00:08:27.989 --> 00:08:32.572
The peace here as a selling
point of emptiness is death,

00:08:36.734 --> 00:08:38.607
rest in peace.

00:08:38.607 --> 00:08:40.190
RIP. Rest in Peace.

00:08:41.607 --> 00:08:46.361
This SHI BA means killed, dead.

00:08:46.361 --> 00:08:48.211
SHI BA in Tibet means dead.

00:08:48.211 --> 00:08:49.931
ZHI BA means peace.

00:08:49.931 --> 00:08:54.931
But he says, "This peace is,
NGO BO NYID KYIS ZHI BA".

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.312
The second selling point for
emptiness is that it is peace

00:09:00.312 --> 00:09:05.229
in the sense that there's no
war going on inside the bottle,

00:09:06.724 --> 00:09:09.474
of thinking it comes from itself.

00:09:12.192 --> 00:09:13.775
It doesn't mean OMmmmmmmmm...

00:09:14.729 --> 00:09:16.371
It doesn't mean that.

00:09:16.371 --> 00:09:20.465
It means the state of
this bottle is peace.

00:09:20.465 --> 00:09:21.298
Why?

00:09:22.369 --> 00:09:24.311
It doesn't have a war inside.

00:09:24.311 --> 00:09:26.925
Why? It doesn't have a self nature.

00:09:26.925 --> 00:09:29.508
And it never had a self nature.

00:09:31.999 --> 00:09:33.375
GDOD MA NAS ZHI BA

00:09:33.375 --> 00:09:37.725
It is peace from the
beginning of no beginning.

00:09:37.725 --> 00:09:40.625
It was always peace. It
never had self-existence.

00:09:40.625 --> 00:09:43.067
You made the war. You put the war here.

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:47.506
It was peace, meaning it
didn't have the war of thinking

00:09:47.506 --> 00:09:50.019
it was self-existence.

00:09:50.019 --> 00:09:54.910
That's cool. Peace from
its own side, self-peace.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:56.046
Well, that's illegal also.

00:09:56.046 --> 00:09:57.910
All right, number three.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.660
DON DAM PAR SPRO BAR BYED
PA'I NGAG GI SPROS PA RNAMS

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.118
KYIS MA SPROS PA STE MA BRJOD PA DANG,

00:10:06.131 --> 00:10:09.903
How do we say this? When
you experience it directly,

00:10:09.903 --> 00:10:11.986
it's not with concepts.

00:10:13.066 --> 00:10:14.072
When you experience it directly

00:10:14.072 --> 00:10:17.417
you're not talking in your head.

00:10:17.417 --> 00:10:18.953
It's a direct communion.

00:10:18.953 --> 00:10:22.760
It's a direct communion
with ultimate reality.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:23.593
SPROS PA means...

00:10:23.593 --> 00:10:27.977
Prapañca means there is no blah blah

00:10:27.977 --> 00:10:29.707
going on in your head.

00:10:29.707 --> 00:10:31.836
It's a direct experience.

00:10:31.836 --> 00:10:34.837
It's free of that kind of blah blah.

00:10:34.837 --> 00:10:37.737
SPROS PA means constructs,

00:10:37.737 --> 00:10:39.866
mental constructs.

00:10:42.702 --> 00:10:43.696
Number four,

00:10:43.696 --> 00:10:46.718
RNAM RTOG GI RGYU BA RNAMS MED

00:10:46.718 --> 00:10:51.073
means when you talk about
the qualities of emptiness,

00:10:51.073 --> 00:10:53.573
an important one is RNAM RTOG.

00:10:55.199 --> 00:10:57.062
RGYU BA means racing through your head.

00:10:57.062 --> 00:11:00.673
It also the word for car race, horse race,

00:11:00.673 --> 00:11:01.506
RGYU BA...

00:11:01.506 --> 00:11:02.985
and RNAM RTOG GI RGYU BA

00:11:02.985 --> 00:11:05.663
means you're not just...

00:11:05.663 --> 00:11:06.830
How do you say?

00:11:08.158 --> 00:11:11.351
You don't have blah blah
thoughts going on in your mind,

00:11:11.351 --> 00:11:13.872
but sometimes blah blah thoughts

00:11:13.872 --> 00:11:17.772
means thinking that the
thing from its own side.

00:11:17.772 --> 00:11:19.928
So that's tricky, here. All right?

00:11:19.928 --> 00:11:21.469
Number five,

00:11:21.469 --> 00:11:23.627
CHOS THAMS CAD KYI DE KHO
NA NYID RIGS MTSUNGS PAS

00:11:23.627 --> 00:11:25.511
DON THA DAD PA MIN PA STE

00:11:27.338 --> 00:11:32.338
Every object in the universe
is as empty as any other object

00:11:32.932 --> 00:11:36.182
and anyone who sees
emptiness once directly,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.707
sees it as well as
anyone ever did or will.

00:11:44.527 --> 00:11:46.735
A Buddha doesn't see emptiness
directly better than an Arya

00:11:46.735 --> 00:11:50.395
for the first time. It's 100%.

00:11:50.395 --> 00:11:52.421
It's totally 100%.

00:11:52.421 --> 00:11:54.941
Then that's that idea that you can't have

00:11:54.941 --> 00:11:56.840
the decrease of emptiness.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:00.931
There can't a 40% mouse not
in the kitchen or something.

00:12:00.931 --> 00:12:02.564
Either it there or it's not there.

00:12:02.564 --> 00:12:05.371
If it's not there it's 100% emptiness.

00:12:05.371 --> 00:12:07.788
If you see emptiness directly

00:12:08.777 --> 00:12:12.763
at age 20 or something as a human being,

00:12:12.763 --> 00:12:16.518
you see it exactly the
same way as Buddha does,

00:12:16.518 --> 00:12:19.221
no different. It's interesting.

00:12:19.221 --> 00:12:21.804
Those are selling points.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:27.113
Having discussed the
features that emptiness has,

00:12:27.113 --> 00:12:31.569
he goes into a little bit
about the features that things

00:12:31.569 --> 00:12:34.986
don't have and the picture was keystroke.

00:12:39.404 --> 00:12:43.154
Yes, and I keep talking
about seeds and sprouts,

00:12:45.757 --> 00:12:47.352
but the number of friends I have

00:12:47.352 --> 00:12:50.602
who really understand gardening is one.

00:12:52.422 --> 00:12:54.003
I mean, we don't grow things.

00:12:54.003 --> 00:12:57.282
When I was a kid I honestly
believed that watermelons,

00:12:57.282 --> 00:12:59.650
sandia, came from the back of the store.

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:00.483
I thought that.

00:13:00.483 --> 00:13:02.614
I thought they made them
in the back of the store.

00:13:02.614 --> 00:13:04.985
So we don't live in an
agricultural society,

00:13:04.985 --> 00:13:06.463
it's like the Bible, right?

00:13:06.463 --> 00:13:11.122
We don't live in a society
where we do much growing things,

00:13:11.122 --> 00:13:13.404
but we do keystrokes.

00:13:13.404 --> 00:13:15.245
So to think that when your finger

00:13:15.245 --> 00:13:17.745
comes down the screen changes,

00:13:18.862 --> 00:13:20.362
to believe that...

00:13:23.016 --> 00:13:25.248
Choney Lama does a cute thing,

00:13:25.248 --> 00:13:28.284
he says, "You know what?
Nagarjuna is jumping

00:13:28.284 --> 00:13:32.749
"from the five qualities
that emptiness does have,

00:13:32.749 --> 00:13:36.299
to the one quality that nothing has."

00:13:36.299 --> 00:13:39.660
And isn't that cool? So he's
seeing a connection where

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.423
maybe he's making it up in
a way, but aren't we always?

00:13:44.423 --> 00:13:48.310
He's like, "Here's the five
great features of emptiness

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:51.811
"but, by the way, there's things you think

00:13:51.811 --> 00:13:54.867
are features of things that aren't."

00:13:54.867 --> 00:13:59.152
Like what? Like thinking
that you make the letter

00:13:59.152 --> 00:14:03.359
on the screen by hitting
the key with your finger.

00:14:03.359 --> 00:14:06.070
Hitting the key with your finger

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:09.291
doesn't bring the letter on the screen.

00:14:09.291 --> 00:14:12.291
That's how we would say it nowadays.

00:14:13.528 --> 00:14:16.890
That connection between keystroke

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:21.223
and something changing on
the screen is not natural,

00:14:23.495 --> 00:14:27.406
which you will discover
whenever your computer breaks.

00:14:27.406 --> 00:14:32.047
You make lots of keystrokes
and nothing happens.

00:14:32.047 --> 00:14:34.526
That connection is not natural.

00:14:34.526 --> 00:14:38.255
That's also coming from
you and it can wear out,

00:14:38.255 --> 00:14:40.356
and then your computer breaks.

00:14:40.356 --> 00:14:45.356
And then you're pissed off
because you lose half your stuff.

00:14:45.542 --> 00:14:49.459
Fifth major section of this chapter.

00:14:50.351 --> 00:14:54.087
DON DE NGES PAR BSGRUB
DGOS PAR BSTAN PA'O,,

00:14:54.087 --> 00:14:58.170
He says, "Don't just study
emptiness. Do something."

00:15:00.325 --> 00:15:02.568
BSGRUB DGOS means 'do something'.

00:15:02.568 --> 00:15:04.656
You've got to do something.

00:15:04.656 --> 00:15:06.824
Don't just study emptiness.

00:15:06.824 --> 00:15:08.740
Now you got to do something with it

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.073
and he's got a picture here.

00:15:12.762 --> 00:15:16.095
I call this picture 'plant seeds for Joe.'

00:15:18.678 --> 00:15:19.705
- [Student] For the next guy.

00:15:19.705 --> 00:15:21.694
- Yes, for the next guy.

00:15:21.694 --> 00:15:25.275
We're talking about putting
aside retirement money today.

00:15:25.275 --> 00:15:28.275
It's just polite to your
elder self, older self.

00:15:30.501 --> 00:15:32.408
Put some money aside for...

00:15:32.408 --> 00:15:36.408
And what happened today?
I forgot what I was doing.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.115
I was being grateful that my earlier self

00:15:40.115 --> 00:15:42.308
did something for me.

00:15:42.308 --> 00:15:44.058
The database I guess.

00:15:45.277 --> 00:15:50.194
Michael at 30 was getting books
typed in for Michael at 60.

00:15:54.227 --> 00:15:55.216
You see what I mean?

00:15:55.216 --> 00:15:59.970
And I was thinking, "Good
job Michael at 30. Thank you!

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:01.795
"Thanks for thinking of me.

00:16:01.795 --> 00:16:04.452
Thanks for giving me a database."

00:16:04.452 --> 00:16:06.702
So it's the same idea here.

00:16:09.015 --> 00:16:13.297
When you study emptiness,
it's not just for you.

00:16:13.297 --> 00:16:15.177
You're taking care of your future self.

00:16:15.177 --> 00:16:18.401
It's like putting aside retirement money.

00:16:18.401 --> 00:16:21.838
Your future self will thank you.

00:16:21.838 --> 00:16:25.032
And I will challenge you guys.

00:16:25.032 --> 00:16:28.511
He said a pretty awesome thing here.

00:16:28.511 --> 00:16:31.614
At this point in the text he
said a pretty awesome thing.

00:16:31.614 --> 00:16:34.468
He said, "If you do take
care of your future self,

00:16:34.468 --> 00:16:38.885
"if you do study emptiness
from the Nagarjuna courses,

00:16:40.738 --> 00:16:42.565
then you're taking care
of your future self,"

00:16:42.565 --> 00:16:43.779
because...

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:46.735
I'll keep giving you
clues if nobody can guess.

00:16:46.735 --> 00:16:48.540
- [Student] If you plant better seeds.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:49.708
- Come on...

00:16:49.708 --> 00:16:52.040
- [student mumbling]

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:55.040
- Yes, even if you were scheduled to die

00:16:57.181 --> 00:16:59.354
and go to a world with no dharma,

00:16:59.354 --> 00:17:02.771
you will create the dharma in that world.

00:17:03.908 --> 00:17:05.605
That's cool.

00:17:05.605 --> 00:17:08.610
That's pretty cool. Got it?

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:11.574
What specific practitioner
was he talking about?

00:17:11.574 --> 00:17:12.407
- [Student] Self-made.

00:17:12.407 --> 00:17:16.030
- Self-made Buddhas. Yes,
self-made Buddhas. Good.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:20.697
That's amazing. I've never
seen anyone say that so cool.

00:17:21.664 --> 00:17:24.773
What's another reason why
you have to study emptiness?

00:17:24.773 --> 00:17:26.681
You got to take care of Joe.

00:17:26.681 --> 00:17:29.702
Who's Joe? Joe is your next life.

00:17:29.702 --> 00:17:33.452
Send him a gift. Send
him some stuff to study

00:17:35.374 --> 00:17:40.215
in the next life. And
it's a very sweet feeling

00:17:40.215 --> 00:17:44.798
when you consciously do
something for your future self,

00:17:46.211 --> 00:17:50.005
like 10 years from now,
and you do something to...

00:17:50.005 --> 00:17:53.560
You kind of send a present to
yourself 10 years from now,

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:55.771
but this time you're doing to yourself

00:17:55.771 --> 00:17:58.334
a hundred years from now,
a thousand years from now.

00:17:58.334 --> 00:18:03.167
And you can make it a Buddhist
practice to do cool things

00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:09.250
that send anonymous gifts
to your future self.

00:18:10.234 --> 00:18:14.051
That's so cool. That's such a nice idea.

00:18:14.051 --> 00:18:17.470
It's like putting chocolate
in drawers at your house

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:20.156
and forgetting about it.
Then later you're like,

00:18:20.156 --> 00:18:24.739
"Oh my God, what's in
there? Thank you Michael

00:18:24.739 --> 00:18:26.845
three months ago."

00:18:26.845 --> 00:18:28.928
That's a cool idea.

00:18:30.456 --> 00:18:33.623
What's our time frame here?

00:18:38.035 --> 00:18:40.952
I stop at 5:05? Okay, got it.

00:18:42.235 --> 00:18:44.667
Plant seeds for Joe. Guess what,

00:18:44.667 --> 00:18:47.067
we finished the review of chapter 18.

00:18:47.067 --> 00:18:50.338
It's kind of slow but that's our theme.

00:18:50.338 --> 00:18:54.171
And that's actually the
most important chapter

00:18:55.683 --> 00:18:58.151
that we covered, is 18

00:18:58.151 --> 00:19:01.818
on the emptiness of me and mine. Good.

00:19:04.295 --> 00:19:06.948
Then we went on to talk about what?

00:19:06.948 --> 00:19:11.948
Do you remember chapter 19? Time. Yes.

00:19:11.948 --> 00:19:16.178
I was like writing these
review notes for myself,

00:19:16.178 --> 00:19:20.059
and I said, "They're going
to have to figure out

00:19:20.059 --> 00:19:23.563
"why that order of the
chapters is what it is.

00:19:23.563 --> 00:19:26.221
"Maybe I can just give them some hints.

00:19:26.221 --> 00:19:28.556
"And I'll give them hints from what

00:19:28.556 --> 00:19:32.474
"Choney Lama says in the
opening of the 19th chapter

00:19:32.474 --> 00:19:35.125
to connect it to the 18th chapter."

00:19:35.125 --> 00:19:36.042
And guess what, he doesn't.

00:19:37.168 --> 00:19:39.755
He doesn't even try.

00:19:39.755 --> 00:19:44.713
So why you get time coming, an
investigation of time coming

00:19:44.713 --> 00:19:47.050
after an investigation of me and mine,

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:48.788
he doesn't even guess.

00:19:48.788 --> 00:19:52.117
He just jumps into it.
So it's up to you guys.

00:19:52.117 --> 00:19:57.117
If you don't do it...maybe
Choney Lama left it for you.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:58.163
All right.

00:19:59.125 --> 00:20:01.554
We learn some Sanskrit at that point.

00:20:01.554 --> 00:20:02.617
Kāla?

00:20:02.617 --> 00:20:03.450
- [Student] Time.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:07.783
- Time. Related to
accelerator, car and all that.

00:20:09.182 --> 00:20:14.182
And then in the same class
we went to the next chapter

00:20:14.255 --> 00:20:17.083
which was already talking
about convergences of causes

00:20:17.083 --> 00:20:20.999
and conditions and the
important word there is hetu.

00:20:20.999 --> 00:20:24.115
If you study Buddhist logic,

00:20:24.115 --> 00:20:27.293
hetu is the single most important word

00:20:27.293 --> 00:20:30.210
and it means 'reason' or 'cause'.

00:20:32.526 --> 00:20:35.839
In this discussion it means cause.

00:20:35.839 --> 00:20:39.196
But it's extremely important in logic

00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:43.196
because it means reason, as a cause.

00:20:45.015 --> 00:20:47.598
So we talked about those words.

00:20:50.523 --> 00:20:54.856
Then the whole theme of the
time chapter was triads,

00:20:57.105 --> 00:20:59.457
groups of three,
GSUM TSAN

00:20:59.457 --> 00:21:03.205
The whole theme of the
time chapter was triads.

00:21:03.205 --> 00:21:06.288
Do you guys remember the first triad?

00:21:10.694 --> 00:21:14.592
- [Student] Yes, it was
like if something exists...

00:21:14.592 --> 00:21:16.040
- No, don't make it...

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:17.517
Past, present, future.

00:21:17.517 --> 00:21:19.290
Just past, present, future.

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:22.950
And then he said, "Look
when you discuss a triad,

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.842
"a natural thing to do
is to compare two to one,

00:21:25.842 --> 00:21:27.971
"is not comparing one to one."

00:21:27.971 --> 00:21:29.810
If you're talking about three things,

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.914
well, how do these two relate to this one?

00:21:31.914 --> 00:21:35.106
How do these two relate to this one?

00:21:35.106 --> 00:21:37.868
So what is the first two
that it lumps together?

00:21:37.868 --> 00:21:41.951
How do the present and
future relate to the past?

00:21:48.446 --> 00:21:53.446
And how can the present and
future even relate to the past?

00:21:53.686 --> 00:21:57.093
It never touches them. They
never touch each other.

00:21:57.093 --> 00:22:00.742
So how did fish relate to green drinks?

00:22:00.742 --> 00:22:03.360
Sorry, that's almost connected.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.040
[student Laughing]

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.457
JB, give me something random.

00:22:09.419 --> 00:22:10.374
[student Mumbling]

00:22:10.374 --> 00:22:13.759
How do the javalinas
wandering around on your lawn

00:22:13.759 --> 00:22:14.926
relate to green drink?

00:22:14.926 --> 00:22:17.880
I mean, they don't a connection.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.593
They can't touch each other.

00:22:19.593 --> 00:22:22.298
The present and the future
cannot touch the past,

00:22:22.298 --> 00:22:23.940
but without the past, the
present and the future

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:25.761
have no meaning.

00:22:25.761 --> 00:22:27.104
They rely on the past.

00:22:27.104 --> 00:22:30.687
The present and the
future rest on the past

00:22:31.538 --> 00:22:33.205
without touching it.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.502
- That's all just made up in your head.

00:22:37.502 --> 00:22:39.723
- Ah, that's correct.

00:22:41.338 --> 00:22:44.921
So technically, the
present and the future,

00:22:46.973 --> 00:22:50.802
how they happen, is going to
depend on what happened before,

00:22:50.802 --> 00:22:54.927
but they have no connection
to what happened before. Why?

00:22:54.927 --> 00:22:57.567
- [student mumbling]

00:22:57.567 --> 00:22:59.809
- Well that's good. Yes, the past is gone.

00:22:59.809 --> 00:23:01.984
How can you have relationship
with somebody who's dead.

00:23:01.984 --> 00:23:03.067
I don't know.

00:23:04.418 --> 00:23:08.999
Then also what? Point of
contact, point of contact.

00:23:08.999 --> 00:23:12.640
What's the point of contact
between nothing and something?

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.168
And even if the past did exist now,

00:23:15.168 --> 00:23:18.168
and even the present does exist now,

00:23:19.661 --> 00:23:21.067
where do they meet?

00:23:21.067 --> 00:23:24.617
- [Student] They touch, they
have to be the same thing.

00:23:24.617 --> 00:23:27.617
- Yes, if the present touches the past,

00:23:28.763 --> 00:23:32.680
then that part of the
past would be the present.

00:23:33.675 --> 00:23:37.576
It's messed up. Something
else must be going on.

00:23:37.576 --> 00:23:40.982
All right, we had calender
pictures, I don't know.

00:23:40.982 --> 00:23:43.512
We don't really need it,
but... You get the point.

00:23:43.512 --> 00:23:47.810
He plays with '22 and
'23 depending on '21.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:51.553
And then says, well do
'21 and '22 depend on '23?

00:23:51.553 --> 00:23:55.389
And maybe '21 and '23 need
'22 nested in the middle

00:23:55.389 --> 00:23:56.968
to make sense?

00:23:56.968 --> 00:23:59.954
He looks at the triads in different ways.

00:23:59.954 --> 00:24:04.322
What else did he say
about groups of three?

00:24:04.322 --> 00:24:07.838
- [student mumbling]

00:24:07.838 --> 00:24:09.145
- Yes, any group of three you ever have,

00:24:09.145 --> 00:24:10.579
just use it for that.

00:24:10.579 --> 00:24:13.592
I think he gave two examples.

00:24:13.592 --> 00:24:15.028
- [Student] Numbers.

00:24:15.028 --> 00:24:17.599
- Like one, two, three. Yes.
In fact they were so simple,

00:24:17.599 --> 00:24:20.332
I didn't understand the Tibetan.

00:24:20.332 --> 00:24:22.164
I was like 'one, two, three?..'

00:24:22.164 --> 00:24:25.393
[students and teacher Laughing]

00:24:25.393 --> 00:24:26.701
And then he said,

00:24:26.701 --> 00:24:29.342
MCHOG DMAN 'BRING

00:24:30.234 --> 00:24:33.234
MCHOG DMAN 'BRING

00:24:34.068 --> 00:24:36.922
Better, worse, in-between.

00:24:36.922 --> 00:24:39.106
Good, bad, in-between.

00:24:39.106 --> 00:24:41.160
That they also depend on each other.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:44.243
But where does good touch in-between?

00:24:45.597 --> 00:24:49.469
Because is that part good
or is that part in-between?

00:24:49.469 --> 00:24:50.928
They can't touch each other.

00:24:50.928 --> 00:24:52.803
If they don't touch each
other you can't compare them,

00:24:52.803 --> 00:24:54.424
and you can't say this chocolate

00:24:54.424 --> 00:24:55.793
is better than that chocolate.

00:24:55.793 --> 00:25:00.535
If they're never in the same
room or in the same mouth.

00:25:00.535 --> 00:25:02.535
We'll experiment.

00:25:07.645 --> 00:25:11.872
Then he asks a question,
idim R.15, if you got it.

00:25:11.872 --> 00:25:15.372
I think it's a person
jumping. And he said,

00:25:16.385 --> 00:25:20.052
"Which happens first,
the future or the past?"

00:25:25.814 --> 00:25:29.149
- [Student] Both. And one
answer is 'past is first,'

00:25:29.149 --> 00:25:30.747
and the other answer is 'future is first.'

00:25:30.747 --> 00:25:35.581
- Yes, good, and what's coming up next

00:25:35.581 --> 00:25:37.906
is the future. Right?

00:25:37.906 --> 00:25:40.073
But then it's the present.

00:25:41.848 --> 00:25:44.602
But the time you looked at it, it's past.

00:25:44.602 --> 00:25:49.285
So our idea of time is messed up.

00:25:51.277 --> 00:25:55.944
I spend a long time
thinking about relativity

00:25:56.824 --> 00:26:00.515
and Einstein and stretching
time, shrinking time,

00:26:00.515 --> 00:26:05.491
bending time, and it's a lot
easier to understand Einstein

00:26:05.491 --> 00:26:08.982
if you read this part,
about the emptiness of time.

00:26:08.982 --> 00:26:12.049
Then it makes a lot of sense.

00:26:24.086 --> 00:26:26.919
I'll show you another picture, 16.

00:26:37.375 --> 00:26:39.713
It's just a personal suggestion.

00:26:39.713 --> 00:26:44.436
When you're trying to
understand emptiness better,

00:26:44.436 --> 00:26:47.769
instead of using the word self-existent,

00:26:49.182 --> 00:26:53.045
it's like trying on
jeans: see what fits you.

00:26:53.045 --> 00:26:54.414
I always tell the story that me and V

00:26:54.414 --> 00:26:57.402
went to the cowboy store in Cottonwood

00:26:57.402 --> 00:27:00.865
and she tried on 42 pairs of jeans

00:27:00.865 --> 00:27:05.698
and didn't buy any of them.
And that guy was a little hot.

00:27:06.674 --> 00:27:10.599
I went back and bought some
boots just to make him happy.

00:27:10.599 --> 00:27:13.849
But try on different words that fit you

00:27:15.272 --> 00:27:18.073
and see which one helps you understand it.

00:27:18.073 --> 00:27:21.255
The word 'natural' is a good suggestion.

00:27:21.255 --> 00:27:22.830
Is it all natural?

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:27.568
Is the way that time
flows part of "nature"?

00:27:27.568 --> 00:27:30.268
Or is it artificial?

00:27:30.268 --> 00:27:32.766
Is it natural or is it artificial?

00:27:32.766 --> 00:27:35.291
You know, they put stuff in candy bars,

00:27:35.291 --> 00:27:40.291
is it natural sugar or is it fake sugar?

00:27:40.562 --> 00:27:44.001
And just when you're trying to think about

00:27:44.001 --> 00:27:46.695
what emptiness means, I
suggest that sometimes

00:27:46.695 --> 00:27:50.970
you think about the idea of
natural and what does it mean.

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:55.970
Is the division into past,
present and future natural?

00:27:57.147 --> 00:27:58.780
And is the nature of time...

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:03.529
Is time automatically
past, present and future

00:28:03.529 --> 00:28:06.558
or is there other ways to look at time?

00:28:06.558 --> 00:28:10.738
And I just encourage you to
think about the word natural

00:28:10.738 --> 00:28:12.821
instead of self-existing.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:18.553
By the way I test out words,
like that audience I mentioned,

00:28:18.553 --> 00:28:20.624
'China Soft Power' people.

00:28:20.624 --> 00:28:25.574
They're very intelligent
business owners, big businesses.

00:28:27.087 --> 00:28:31.122
And I say, "Look, let
me ask you something,

00:28:31.122 --> 00:28:33.877
"I'm a teacher, I spend a lot
of time, I go around the world

00:28:33.877 --> 00:28:35.785
"trying to teach people

00:28:35.785 --> 00:28:37.409
and I have a question for you guys."

00:28:37.409 --> 00:28:40.300
They're like, "Run it."

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:45.300
I say, "Should I say, karma
or should I say mental seeds?"

00:28:45.608 --> 00:28:49.285
And they're like, "Mental
seeds, man, forget karma."

00:28:49.285 --> 00:28:52.098
And I was like, I felt so happy

00:28:52.098 --> 00:28:55.834
that they can just give
me an honest opinion.

00:28:55.834 --> 00:28:57.493
They didn't hesitate at all.

00:28:57.493 --> 00:29:00.845
They said, "Oh, f' that. Forget karma.

00:29:00.845 --> 00:29:03.753
Don't mention karma.
Just say mental seeds."

00:29:03.753 --> 00:29:08.375
So I'm suggesting to you:
think about the word.

00:29:08.375 --> 00:29:12.458
Personally, self-existent
doesn't help me at all.

00:29:13.504 --> 00:29:16.764
I never walk down the street and say,

00:29:16.764 --> 00:29:20.561
"Oh, you're looking at
this self-existent..."

00:29:20.561 --> 00:29:24.210
We don't even think of it.

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:28.978
I think one that helps everybody
is 'from its own side'.

00:29:28.978 --> 00:29:31.639
"Is that pen coming from its own side?"

00:29:31.639 --> 00:29:36.464
'Self-existent' doesn't mean
almost anything to anybody.

00:29:36.464 --> 00:29:39.754
But if you say, "Does it
come from its own side?"

00:29:39.754 --> 00:29:42.298
Then they say, "Yes, it does."

00:29:42.298 --> 00:29:47.143
It's very easy way to get
into emptiness discussion.

00:29:47.143 --> 00:29:50.203
And I'm suggesting that
'natural' is a good one too.

00:29:50.203 --> 00:29:53.703
Is the division of time
itself into three...

00:29:54.993 --> 00:29:57.760
Does it have to be that
way? Is that natural?

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:00.962
Or is it something we made up?

00:30:00.962 --> 00:30:02.774
Is it somehow coming from us?

00:30:02.774 --> 00:30:05.160
Because the idea of
past, present and future

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.869
is so much a part of our life

00:30:07.869 --> 00:30:10.153
that we think it must be natural.

00:30:10.153 --> 00:30:12.971
I didn't make up past. I
mean, the idea of past,

00:30:12.971 --> 00:30:15.330
and the idea of future
and the idea of present,

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:19.042
I didn't make those divisions.

00:30:19.042 --> 00:30:22.512
That God made that ort the
world mad that, I don't know.

00:30:22.512 --> 00:30:24.774
That was here when I got here.

00:30:24.774 --> 00:30:28.186
People were already talking
about yesterday and tomorrow.

00:30:28.186 --> 00:30:31.652
And Nagarjuna says, "No, it's not natural.

00:30:31.652 --> 00:30:33.152
You did make it."

00:30:38.484 --> 00:30:40.805
Personally, I'm interested
that there was a new verb

00:30:40.805 --> 00:30:45.805
that I never saw before, it's
called BSNOR BA, B-S-N-O-R

00:30:46.477 --> 00:30:50.977
BSNOR BA, so NOR low tone,
N-O-R, means 'to make a mistake',

00:30:53.153 --> 00:30:56.986
and then he said, "No
matter which of the triad

00:30:59.463 --> 00:31:01.932
you look at depending on the others."

00:31:01.932 --> 00:31:04.722
Does '23 depend on '21 and '22?

00:31:04.722 --> 00:31:07.555
Does 2023 depend on 2021 and 2022?

00:31:09.149 --> 00:31:12.315
Or does 2022 depend '21 and '23?

00:31:12.315 --> 00:31:15.863
Or does '21 depend on '22 and '23?

00:31:15.863 --> 00:31:16.863
Which is it?

00:31:17.812 --> 00:31:18.812
And he says,

00:31:19.657 --> 00:31:23.301
"Well, personally, when
I taught it to you guys,

00:31:23.301 --> 00:31:25.384
"I started with '21, 2021

00:31:26.739 --> 00:31:30.034
"and then I try to show
you how '22 and '23's

00:31:30.034 --> 00:31:32.784
relationship depending on that."

00:31:34.423 --> 00:31:36.631
But it could've been anything.

00:31:36.631 --> 00:31:40.985
We could take anyone and
look at the relationship.

00:31:40.985 --> 00:31:42.402
And then he says,

00:31:44.282 --> 00:31:47.532
"No matter which two you take together,

00:31:48.948 --> 00:31:52.531
and which one you take
separately, BSNOR BA."

00:31:53.697 --> 00:31:54.580
- [Student] BSNOR BA.

00:31:54.580 --> 00:31:59.580
- Yes, it's a cool verb. It
means there will be an f-up.

00:32:03.796 --> 00:32:06.546
It exactly means that. I'm sorry.

00:32:07.872 --> 00:32:10.372
You have to add a few letters.

00:32:11.756 --> 00:32:13.447
[students laughing]

00:32:13.447 --> 00:32:15.893
It means things will
get screwy either way.

00:32:15.893 --> 00:32:17.902
Things are going to get screwy.

00:32:17.902 --> 00:32:20.819
It's a verb. He made up a new verb.

00:32:22.105 --> 00:32:25.188
It's like, it's going to screwify.

00:32:27.086 --> 00:32:28.071
Anyway, it's a new word.

00:32:28.071 --> 00:32:30.908
For me it's very fascinating,
because I love words.

00:32:30.908 --> 00:32:32.947
He says, "Whatever combination you take,

00:32:32.947 --> 00:32:36.370
it's all going to screwify you."

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:39.870
Good luck translating that, you guys.

00:32:41.903 --> 00:32:42.744
All right, let's look at...

00:32:42.744 --> 00:32:44.319
Let's keep going.

00:32:44.319 --> 00:32:46.819
We looked at all the triads.

00:32:50.166 --> 00:32:52.839
Then he went on a side trip,
and I challenge you guys

00:32:52.839 --> 00:32:54.589
to guess what it was.

00:32:55.716 --> 00:32:58.344
He finished this idea of threes.

00:32:58.344 --> 00:33:03.261
He finished the idea of relativity
of time, which is not...

00:33:05.136 --> 00:33:09.097
Relativity is not the same
thing as coming from you.

00:33:09.097 --> 00:33:12.922
The fact that how long the past feels

00:33:12.922 --> 00:33:16.229
depends on how long the
present and future feels

00:33:16.229 --> 00:33:17.646
is not emptiness.

00:33:18.681 --> 00:33:21.952
Relativity is not emptiness.

00:33:21.952 --> 00:33:26.035
The fact that time with
your girlfriend go slower

00:33:28.951 --> 00:33:29.784
because...

00:33:31.231 --> 00:33:33.677
That's not emptiness.

00:33:33.677 --> 00:33:36.234
Relativity is not emptiness.

00:33:37.442 --> 00:33:40.895
It's not true that if there
was no suffering in the world

00:33:40.895 --> 00:33:44.562
you couldn't have
pleasure. That's not true.

00:33:46.025 --> 00:33:48.324
The idea that this is
dependent origination

00:33:48.324 --> 00:33:50.868
which I have heard repeated many times

00:33:50.868 --> 00:33:54.732
by 'Vipassana' people,
is totally mistaken.

00:33:54.732 --> 00:33:56.566
It doesn't mean that at all.

00:33:56.566 --> 00:33:58.072
In that case the Buddha would have to have

00:33:58.072 --> 00:34:03.023
a bit of suffering so he
could enjoy his happiness.

00:34:03.023 --> 00:34:05.039
It's obviously mistaken.

00:34:05.039 --> 00:34:05.945
So emptiness doesn't mean that something

00:34:05.945 --> 00:34:10.580
is something only by comparison.

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:13.512
Emptiness means you're
creating all the degrees,

00:34:13.512 --> 00:34:16.442
whether you see degrees
or you don't see degrees

00:34:16.442 --> 00:34:21.442
in better, worse, medium.
They're all coming from you.

00:34:21.712 --> 00:34:22.572
That's the point.

00:34:22.572 --> 00:34:24.981
It's not that you can't
have better without worse.

00:34:24.981 --> 00:34:28.792
If that's true then you should
just jump off a building

00:34:28.792 --> 00:34:30.632
because you can't have happiness.

00:34:30.632 --> 00:34:32.970
You can't have pure happiness.

00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:35.258
You'd have to go out and
get a dose of unhappiness

00:34:35.258 --> 00:34:39.222
so you could appreciate your
happiness, something like that.

00:34:39.222 --> 00:34:42.427
Then why am I talking
about like that, I forgot.

00:34:42.427 --> 00:34:43.975
- [Student] Side trip.

00:34:43.975 --> 00:34:46.392
- Yes, what is his side trip?

00:34:47.267 --> 00:34:50.036
What? Guess. He's talking about time

00:34:50.036 --> 00:34:53.615
and he's going on to his next chapter.

00:34:53.615 --> 00:34:56.565
He's ready to go on to his next chapter,

00:34:56.565 --> 00:35:01.250
but he wants to say a
little bit more about time.

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:05.304
He presents other people's ideas of time.

00:35:05.304 --> 00:35:07.554
He stops and takes a break.

00:35:08.573 --> 00:35:10.859
He said, "Hey, you want to
know what the non-Buddhist

00:35:10.859 --> 00:35:13.325
"in ancient India thought about time?

00:35:13.325 --> 00:35:15.770
"Or you want to know what
the lower Buddhist schools

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:17.225
thought about time?"

00:35:17.225 --> 00:35:19.392
And he goes off like that.

00:35:20.336 --> 00:35:23.526
That's a popular strategy for Nagarjuna.

00:35:23.526 --> 00:35:26.359
He often will compare his concepts

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:33.660
to the concepts of other
traditions in his culture,

00:35:33.660 --> 00:35:37.820
which was Hindu, Jain, other traditions.

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:38.987
Many. 'Mīmāṃsā'.

00:35:39.934 --> 00:35:41.731
And then he'll say, "By the way,

00:35:41.731 --> 00:35:43.879
and lower Buddhist
schools think like this."

00:35:43.879 --> 00:35:46.212
And then he'll give you more confidence

00:35:46.212 --> 00:35:50.066
about what your school is by
describing other traditions.

00:35:50.066 --> 00:35:52.066
So here's the first one.

00:35:56.038 --> 00:35:59.788
Somebody came from another
tradition and said,

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.365
"I don't know why you have
all this trouble with time

00:36:05.365 --> 00:36:08.482
"because we have definitions of time.

00:36:08.482 --> 00:36:12.565
"We have seconds. We have
minutes. We have hours.

00:36:13.414 --> 00:36:17.340
"We have weeks. We have
months. We have centuries.

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:20.173
"We have millenia. What's the problem?

00:36:20.173 --> 00:36:23.945
"We've got units of time.
They may be artificial

00:36:23.945 --> 00:36:25.179
"but they're fixed.

00:36:25.179 --> 00:36:29.405
An hour is 60 minutes.
A month is 30, 28 days"

00:36:29.405 --> 00:36:33.988
Well, he says, "A year is 12
months. It may be made up.

00:36:35.046 --> 00:36:38.296
"It may be an artifice, but it's fixed.

00:36:39.909 --> 00:36:42.643
"You don't have to stress
so much about time,

00:36:42.643 --> 00:36:45.743
"being compared to the
other times and other...

00:36:45.743 --> 00:36:48.410
They have a definition."

00:36:48.410 --> 00:36:49.243
And he says,

00:36:49.243 --> 00:36:51.451
"Oh, oh, what's the definition?"

00:36:51.451 --> 00:36:54.328
- One minute is 60.
- So what's a second?

00:36:54.328 --> 00:36:59.278
- A second is like 60
milliseconds, 1000 milliseconds.

00:36:59.278 --> 00:37:01.283
That's all, 1000 milliseconds.

00:37:01.283 --> 00:37:04.286
He says, "Well, oh, I see. So
your years are based on months?"

00:37:04.286 --> 00:37:05.689
- Yeah, you got it.

00:37:05.689 --> 00:37:06.888
- And the months are based on days?

00:37:06.888 --> 00:37:07.795
- Yeah, you got it.

00:37:07.795 --> 00:37:08.744
- The days on hours?

00:37:08.744 --> 00:37:09.803
- Got it.

00:37:09.803 --> 00:37:11.346
- And seconds on milliseconds.

00:37:11.346 --> 00:37:13.502
- How long is a millisecond?

00:37:13.502 --> 00:37:16.265
And then the guy is like,

00:37:16.265 --> 00:37:20.836
"Well, you know it's just &lt;dip&gt;."

00:37:20.836 --> 00:37:22.884
And he says, "Oh, &lt;dip&gt;."

00:37:22.884 --> 00:37:26.615
And then Nagarjuna is like, "How
many &lt;dips&gt; inside the &lt;dips&gt;?"

00:37:26.615 --> 00:37:28.655
And the guy says, "We
know the dip, dip thing,

00:37:28.655 --> 00:37:30.489
dip, dip, dip %thing."

00:37:30.489 --> 00:37:32.078
And then he says, "Well,
how many dips, dips, dips,

00:37:32.078 --> 00:37:32.965
in the dip, dip?"

00:37:32.965 --> 00:37:35.395
And he says, "Well, you
just cut it down to the

00:37:35.395 --> 00:37:38.418
shortest moment of time and
then you grab that one."

00:37:38.418 --> 00:37:40.311
He says, "You can't."

00:37:40.311 --> 00:37:44.591
BZUNG DUS MED PA means you can't grab it.

00:37:44.591 --> 00:37:47.978
It's too short for perception.
You can't perceive it.

00:37:47.978 --> 00:37:51.001
If you get a measurement of time

00:37:51.001 --> 00:37:52.838
which is too short to perceive,

00:37:52.838 --> 00:37:55.073
what does it mean to a human being

00:37:55.073 --> 00:37:57.851
who is a perceiving animal?

00:37:57.851 --> 00:38:00.907
Then it's just you making up stuff.

00:38:00.907 --> 00:38:03.079
Now you're just making up stuff.

00:38:03.079 --> 00:38:08.079
He says, "I agree you can
define a day as so many seconds,

00:38:08.619 --> 00:38:11.027
but what do you define as a second?"

00:38:11.027 --> 00:38:12.710
The guy says, "There
are many milliseconds."

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:16.790
And he says, "What about
the parts of that?"

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:18.970
The guy says, "But you
can't perceive that."

00:38:18.970 --> 00:38:22.507
He says, "What does it mean
to have 50 non perceivables

00:38:22.507 --> 00:38:26.359
"and 60 non perceivables and
then 300 non perceivables?

00:38:26.359 --> 00:38:29.490
"Don't you realize it's all BS?

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:34.315
"You're piling up zeros and
saying it's 50. Got it?"

00:38:34.315 --> 00:38:36.864
Because you can't define the littlest one,

00:38:36.864 --> 00:38:38.207
because you can't perceive it any more,

00:38:38.207 --> 00:38:40.898
because it doesn't have a...

00:38:40.898 --> 00:38:41.731
- [Student] Front end and back end.

00:38:41.731 --> 00:38:45.064
- Front end and back end. Yes.

00:38:45.947 --> 00:38:49.586
It would be nice, your third
book from today's conversation,

00:38:49.586 --> 00:38:51.948
should be front ends and back ends.

00:38:51.948 --> 00:38:53.017
I was thinking you could...

00:38:53.017 --> 00:38:57.975
Well, anyway, we can talk
about that photo on the front..

00:38:57.975 --> 00:39:00.137
[student laughing]

00:39:00.137 --> 00:39:00.970
By the way, here's the picture of...

00:39:03.505 --> 00:39:06.639
Those milliseconds just
kind of run away, fly away.

00:39:06.639 --> 00:39:09.879
You can't define them. You can't...

00:39:09.879 --> 00:39:11.463
BZUNG DUS MED PA means

00:39:11.463 --> 00:39:13.963
they cannot be....'perceptio'.

00:39:15.618 --> 00:39:19.258
They cannot be grasped,
perceived. They cannot be.

00:39:19.258 --> 00:39:21.313
You can't hold them.
You can't define them.

00:39:21.313 --> 00:39:23.563
You can't touch them.

00:39:25.246 --> 00:39:29.522
Your comforting units of
time were made up of zeros.

00:39:29.522 --> 00:39:31.276
And you're piling them
up and saying it's 50,

00:39:31.276 --> 00:39:33.667
but it's still 50 zeros.

00:39:33.667 --> 00:39:35.302
That's all.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:45.253
We want to go deeper?

00:39:45.253 --> 00:39:46.418
- [Students] Yay!

00:39:46.418 --> 00:39:48.238
- How much time we've got?

00:39:48.238 --> 00:39:49.071
- [Student] Five minutes.

00:39:49.071 --> 00:39:50.257
- We still have five?

00:39:50.257 --> 00:39:55.174
Having talked about the
other traditions of his time,

00:39:56.806 --> 00:40:00.230
which by the way, whenever
you see that in scripture,

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:05.230
I'll tell you a clue. You can
pretty much fill in 'science.'

00:40:05.876 --> 00:40:10.782
You don't have to say Jainism
or Samkya system or Mimamsa,

00:40:10.782 --> 00:40:15.406
or Charvaka, you can just say scientist,

00:40:15.406 --> 00:40:18.889
because they're as crude and wrong

00:40:18.889 --> 00:40:22.850
as the non-Buddhist
schools 2000 years ago.

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:26.911
Just fill in the name
'science,' it's okay.

00:40:26.911 --> 00:40:29.319
So he says, "Okay, we
wiped out the scientists.

00:40:29.319 --> 00:40:31.539
Who else we've got?"

00:40:31.539 --> 00:40:33.716
"What about the lower Buddhist schools?"

00:40:33.716 --> 00:40:36.186
"Yes, what about the functionalists?"

00:40:36.186 --> 00:40:39.553
These are the three lower schools.

00:40:39.553 --> 00:40:42.470
They say something interesting.

00:40:45.257 --> 00:40:49.327
Time is measurable and time is definable

00:40:49.327 --> 00:40:54.071
because you can link it to a
physical object like the moon,

00:40:54.071 --> 00:40:58.052
the phases of the moon or the sun setting

00:40:58.052 --> 00:40:59.395
and the sun coming up.

00:40:59.395 --> 00:41:02.218
There are physical things in the world

00:41:02.218 --> 00:41:06.811
and you can tie time to
them and say that's a day.

00:41:06.811 --> 00:41:09.193
When the sun comes up and
when the sun goes down,

00:41:09.193 --> 00:41:11.360
that half circle is a day.

00:41:13.159 --> 00:41:17.159
So the functionalists,
the Buddhist schools say,

00:41:19.092 --> 00:41:22.092
"We can define time in certain units

00:41:24.858 --> 00:41:27.058
"because we can tie those times,

00:41:27.058 --> 00:41:29.377
"we can tie the theory of time

00:41:29.377 --> 00:41:32.351
to physical objects in the
world that we can watch."

00:41:32.351 --> 00:41:36.919
So it takes 30 days for the
moon to shrink and come back,

00:41:36.919 --> 00:41:38.844
and that's fixed amount of time.

00:41:38.844 --> 00:41:41.371
You can just say that way.

00:41:41.371 --> 00:41:44.599
And then he says, "Oh, so..."

00:41:44.599 --> 00:41:46.395
Here it's a little bit subtle.

00:41:46.395 --> 00:41:47.640
I told you we're going to go deeper,

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:51.307
and that's may be the
last thing we do here.

00:41:53.422 --> 00:41:57.226
Anybody want to guess what Nagarjuna says?

00:41:57.226 --> 00:42:00.241
I'll give you a clue.

00:42:00.241 --> 00:42:02.863
The lower Buddhist
guys, they come and say,

00:42:02.863 --> 00:42:05.863
"Look, time is definable externally."

00:42:07.174 --> 00:42:08.007
- Why?

00:42:08.007 --> 00:42:11.547
- You can link it to a physical event

00:42:11.547 --> 00:42:14.880
like the change of the moon
or the appearance of the...

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:18.149
or the stars circling by the next season,

00:42:18.149 --> 00:42:20.453
certain constellations come back.

00:42:20.453 --> 00:42:24.491
These are the three traditional
Vinaya measures of time,

00:42:24.491 --> 00:42:27.857
which are lunar, solar and stellar.

00:42:27.857 --> 00:42:32.857
And Vinaya has all three. It's
very beautiful, but anyway.

00:42:32.857 --> 00:42:35.391
Last thing today.

00:42:35.391 --> 00:42:39.417
I'll say it again. Lower Buddhist schools:

00:42:39.417 --> 00:42:43.545
"We can define time because
we can take a physical

00:42:43.545 --> 00:42:47.401
"thing that we can see in
the sky and we can link that

00:42:47.401 --> 00:42:51.068
"to the thing called
day or night, or month,

00:42:52.292 --> 00:42:55.042
"or season of the constellations.

00:42:56.032 --> 00:42:59.032
"No problem. That's not artificial."

00:43:01.915 --> 00:43:04.907
Okay, last thing.

00:43:04.907 --> 00:43:09.907
$100. How does Nagarjuna argue back?

00:43:10.002 --> 00:43:11.639
I'll say it again.

00:43:11.639 --> 00:43:16.306
They say, measurements of
time can be fixed objectively,

00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:23.220
because you can tie them to
the motion of planetary bodies.

00:43:23.406 --> 00:43:26.989
I'll give you a clue.
I'll give you a clue.

00:43:29.929 --> 00:43:31.020
- [Student] They can't connect.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:34.755
- The connection is coming from you.

00:43:34.755 --> 00:43:37.607
The decision to connect to
that is coming from you.

00:43:37.607 --> 00:43:39.304
So the whole thing is coming from you.

00:43:39.304 --> 00:43:40.222
Got it?

00:43:40.222 --> 00:43:43.568
If one part of the whole
thing is coming from you,

00:43:43.568 --> 00:43:45.117
then it's coming from you.

00:43:45.117 --> 00:43:47.500
- Is there a sun moving in
the sky in a certain way?

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:48.333
- Maybe.

00:43:49.859 --> 00:43:54.249
- Is there words called
minutes, hours, days? Yes.

00:43:54.249 --> 00:43:57.052
- Do the words hours,
minutes, days make sense

00:43:57.052 --> 00:43:59.372
without reference to a physical body?

00:43:59.372 --> 00:44:01.219
- No. You've got to have a physical body.

00:44:01.219 --> 00:44:02.993
- Okay. So, it's artificial?

00:44:02.993 --> 00:44:03.826
- No, it's not.

00:44:03.826 --> 00:44:04.659
- Yes, it is.

00:44:04.659 --> 00:44:05.492
- Why?

00:44:05.492 --> 00:44:07.660
- You decided what to connect to what.

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:09.405
You decided what to connect to what.

00:44:09.405 --> 00:44:13.379
Therefore, it's coming from you. Got it?

00:44:14.569 --> 00:44:16.052
Who made a 100$? Rosa, yes.

00:44:16.052 --> 00:44:16.893
- [Student] Rosa.

00:44:16.893 --> 00:44:19.601
- I think it was Rosa.

00:44:19.601 --> 00:44:22.087
We brought into question

00:44:22.087 --> 00:44:24.978
the very concept of time itself.

00:44:24.978 --> 00:44:26.468
And that's going to naturally

00:44:26.468 --> 00:44:29.808
lead into a discussion of the time

00:44:29.808 --> 00:44:33.686
it takes for results to come
out of collections of causes

00:44:33.686 --> 00:44:36.418
and conditions and then
that's going to naturally

00:44:36.418 --> 00:44:39.940
relate to how do things
come into existence

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:42.186
and how do things leave existence.

00:44:42.186 --> 00:44:45.168
So there's a flow there. If you heard me,

00:44:45.168 --> 00:44:48.272
you can do part of your homework.

00:44:48.272 --> 00:44:51.355
And again, I just encourage you to...

00:44:53.104 --> 00:44:58.079
I agree that studying emptiness all day

00:45:00.106 --> 00:45:03.037
and not helping people in need,

00:45:03.037 --> 00:45:06.165
not helping refugees or poor people

00:45:06.165 --> 00:45:09.390
or people who are at war...

00:45:10.308 --> 00:45:14.558
I agree that that's unwise
and that you're probably

00:45:15.418 --> 00:45:18.654
not developing spiritually. I agree.

00:45:18.654 --> 00:45:21.867
And I agree that if you have no compassion

00:45:21.867 --> 00:45:25.498
or love for other people
then you're failure

00:45:25.498 --> 00:45:28.281
in your spiritual... I agree.

00:45:28.281 --> 00:45:30.380
And I agree that if you're
not doing your exercise

00:45:30.380 --> 00:45:33.216
and your yoga or things like that,

00:45:33.216 --> 00:45:37.415
you are going to hurt
yourself. You can't just sit

00:45:37.415 --> 00:45:40.471
and study emptiness all day. I agree.

00:45:40.471 --> 00:45:45.010
But if you don't spend some quality time,

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:47.240
and I keep saying that
to my close friends.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:49.222
They say, "Oh, Geshe-la,

00:45:49.222 --> 00:45:51.914
I look at the books
once a while, I'm okay."

00:45:51.914 --> 00:45:54.521
And I say, "Yes, but do
you give it quality time?

00:45:54.521 --> 00:45:55.895
Do you give it your..."

00:45:55.895 --> 00:46:00.450
For me my quality time is
like 8:00AM to 11:00AM.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.105
That's the quality time.

00:46:03.105 --> 00:46:07.077
And I say, "Do you consciously
give it your quality time?"

00:46:07.077 --> 00:46:09.994
And they say, "Well, I don't know."

00:46:11.059 --> 00:46:14.504
So, what I'm saying is studying emptiness

00:46:14.504 --> 00:46:18.870
is not the only thing to
do to become a good person,

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:22.108
but it's a necessary part
of becoming a good person.

00:46:22.108 --> 00:46:26.729
And if you skip it because
it seems intellectual,

00:46:26.729 --> 00:46:28.728
then you're making a huge mistake.

00:46:28.728 --> 00:46:33.728
You got to use the parts of your mind

00:46:33.745 --> 00:46:38.745
that are not your physical
senses to see deeper things.

00:46:38.767 --> 00:46:43.767
You have to develop your mind
as a instrument of perception

00:46:44.081 --> 00:46:45.982
to see deeper things.

00:46:45.982 --> 00:46:49.899
If you don't practice
thinking about emptiness,

00:46:51.343 --> 00:46:54.318
you won't see emptiness.

00:46:54.318 --> 00:46:56.318
It's not possible. It won't happen.

00:46:56.318 --> 00:47:00.289
This a muscle also. You
got to exercise your eyes.

00:47:00.289 --> 00:47:03.089
There's a yoga exercising with
your eyes. Do you know that?

00:47:03.089 --> 00:47:03.922
- [Students] Yes.

00:47:03.922 --> 00:47:06.907
- You go like this. It actually helps.

00:47:06.907 --> 00:47:09.019
And you can develop your hearing.

00:47:09.019 --> 00:47:11.358
You can work out in the gym.

00:47:11.358 --> 00:47:15.358
But to think that you don't
have to exercise your mind

00:47:15.358 --> 00:47:18.120
to have a happy life is big mistake.

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:20.120
So dig into these ideas.

00:47:22.748 --> 00:47:27.706
The big, big break-throughs
are with friends.

00:47:30.272 --> 00:47:34.241
And that's why Buddhist monks
spend two, three hours a day

00:47:34.241 --> 00:47:35.191
in a debate ground.

00:47:35.191 --> 00:47:39.553
And you see pictures of the form,

00:47:39.553 --> 00:47:43.468
of the people jumping
around and screaming,

00:47:43.468 --> 00:47:47.662
but the big break-throughs are
the whispered conversations

00:47:47.662 --> 00:47:51.199
in the hours after, in the debate ground.

00:47:51.199 --> 00:47:52.897
The people sit down and they're like,

00:47:52.897 --> 00:47:54.590
"God, I don't get it,"

00:47:54.590 --> 00:47:56.030
and the other guy says,
"I don't get it either,"

00:47:56.030 --> 00:47:59.347
and then third guy says,
"I kind of got it."

00:47:59.347 --> 00:48:02.426
And then you see these

00:48:02.426 --> 00:48:04.558
movies of debates, right?

00:48:04.558 --> 00:48:07.088
But there is a big part
of the debate ground

00:48:07.088 --> 00:48:10.770
where you sit down, when
you're all exhausted

00:48:10.770 --> 00:48:13.970
and you start talking. We are in the dark.

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:16.953
We are in the star light
and it's so beautiful.

00:48:16.953 --> 00:48:18.404
You just start saying,

00:48:18.404 --> 00:48:21.241
"Could you really get that
thing? I didn't get it."

00:48:21.241 --> 00:48:26.183
So it's crucial that you
meet with your friends.

00:48:27.371 --> 00:48:29.337
You meet with other students.

00:48:29.337 --> 00:48:32.409
Don't talk about stupid, useless things.

00:48:32.409 --> 00:48:34.866
If you want to be the Bodhisattva

00:48:34.866 --> 00:48:38.533
in your group of friends,
you be the one who,

00:48:39.449 --> 00:48:43.039
when the conversation at
dinner starts to deteriorate

00:48:43.039 --> 00:48:45.456
into politics or some gossip,

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:48.949
then say, "God, what did you
think about that chapter 21,

00:48:48.949 --> 00:48:50.398
did you get it?"

00:48:50.398 --> 00:48:54.748
And it's so fun. You will
trigger a conversation.

00:48:54.748 --> 00:48:57.822
And it's nice to be the one who secretly

00:48:57.822 --> 00:48:59.223
triggers the conversation.

00:48:59.223 --> 00:49:04.223
So, try to be that kind of person who,

00:49:04.479 --> 00:49:05.677
when you're with your friends,

00:49:05.677 --> 00:49:10.677
especially your dharma
friends, try to secretly

00:49:10.963 --> 00:49:14.491
push the conversation to
something about emptiness

00:49:14.491 --> 00:49:18.460
or something meaningful.
That's a wonderful skill.

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:21.303
That should be one of the perfections:

00:49:21.303 --> 00:49:24.630
how to push the conversation
to something useful.

00:49:24.630 --> 00:49:27.248
So do that and if you do that

00:49:27.248 --> 00:49:29.046
you will understand Nagarjuna.

00:49:29.046 --> 00:49:30.984
And luckily we will have
a couple more sessions

00:49:30.984 --> 00:49:32.711
of Nagarjuna.

00:49:32.711 --> 00:49:33.544
See you then.

00:49:37.652 --> 00:49:40.785
Yay!
[students auplauding]

